WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.210
In the previous part of this lesson, we saw
what ‘refraction of light’ is!

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.170
It’s
simply the bending of light when it travels

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.750
from one medium to another!

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.490
The
question I left you with was “Why does light

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:21.450
change its direction after
entering another medium at an angle?”

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.640
Let’s discuss that in detail.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.270
The change in direction of light occurs because
of

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.720
the CHANGE IN THE SPEED of light in THAT MEDIUM.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.780
When light enters another medium, there is
a CHANGE IN ITS SPEED.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.399
The
speed may either increase or decrease depending

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:39.370
on the medium.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.090
Note that
when we say that the speed will increase,

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.230
it does NOT mean that the speed will
be greater than THIS value, which is the speed

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.230
of light in vacuum.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.230
It means that
its speed in the refractive medium will be

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.230
greater than its speed in the incident
medium.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.230
Similarly, the decrease in speed is also in
comparison with the speed

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.230
in the incident medium.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.350
When the light is entering into the glass
at an angle, it travels at a lesser speed

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:58.690
than in air... and when it goes from glass
to air, its speed increases.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.829
So it is because of this change in speed that
the direction of light changes!

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:06.950
But what if light is entering PERPENDICULAR
to the surface of the glass?

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.540
Will its
speed change?

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.780
Yes, its speed will change, however it does
not bend this time.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.229
Yes, the refracted ray propagates in the same
direction as the incident ray.

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:24.250
Talking about the case when light enters at
an angle… there is one more

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.759
observation that we can make.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:29.210
Observe this part of the refraction.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.540
Observe the incident and refracted rays!

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.009
We notice that the refracted ray is more towards
the normal than the incident

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:37.009
ray.

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:40.670
When the light ray enters the glass from air,
it bends… and it bends

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.810
TOWARDS the normal.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.630
And from this refraction, we can see that
when the light

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.310
ray again enters the air, it bends AWAY from
the normal.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:54.359
So we can say that when the light travels
from an OPTICALLY RARER MEDIUM

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:59.140
to an OPTICALLY DENSER MEDIUM, it bends TOWARDS
the normal and when

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.829
the light travels from an optically denser
medium to an optically rarer medium,

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:05.061
it bends AWAY from the normal.

00:02:05.061 --> 00:02:06.061
Optically rarer to Optically denser  Bends
towards the normal

00:02:06.061 --> 00:02:07.229
Optically denser to Optically rarer  Bends
away from the normal

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:11.320
What do we mean by optically rarer and denser
mediums by the way?

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.390
An optical medium is a medium through which
light can propagate.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.660
Since both
air and glass are transparent, light can pass

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.910
through them.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.450
So,both of them are
optical mediums.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.080
What about rarer and denser?

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.440
If the refractive medium is optically rarer
compared to the incident medium, then the

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.710
speed of light in it will be greater!

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.460
And when the refractive medium is optically
denser compared to the incident

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.570
medium, the speed of light in it will be lesser.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.220
Note than in both cases, we are
comparing the speed of light with the speed

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.810
of light in the incident medium!

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.230
So what do we understand by this?

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.310
How can we summarise it?

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.090
When light is travelling from an optically
rarer medium to an optically denser

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:01.000
medium, its speed will decrease and it will
bend TOWARDS the normal.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.380
Whereas, when the light is travelling from
an optically denser medium to an

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.540
optically rarer medium, its speed will increase
and it will bend AWAY from the

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:10.540
normal.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:11.540
Optically rarer to Optically denser  Speed
decreases , Bends towards the normal

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.540
Optically denser to Optically rarer  Speed
indcreases , Bends away from the normal

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.570
Now that this is clear, I want you to understand
another concept!

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:20.200
Now instead of a glass slab, we have water,
and also suppose that the ray of

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.560
light is incident in the same direction.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.370
We can see that this angle is the same in
both the cases!

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.810
So will the refracted ray in this case travel
in this direction only?

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.510
In other
words, will the angle of refraction be the

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.760
same in both cases?

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.750
No the angle of refraction will be different
in this case.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.430
In fact the angle of
refraction here is greater than the previous

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.560
one.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.020
So what can we infer?

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.070
We can say that the refraction of a light
ray depends on

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.970
the MATERIAL MEDIUM on which light is incident.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.400
Yes, light rays will be
refracted by different amounts in different

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.400
materials.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.930
But wait…

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.290
I have a couple of questions for you!

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.280
Talking about the case when
light enters at an angle… how does the change

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.820
in speed result in the change in
direction of light?

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.620
And why does this perpendicular ray not change
its direction

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.170
after refraction?

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:20.030
I know that you must be curious to know the
answers!

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.750
However we’ll understand this in the next
video.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.049
It’s really interesting, make
sure you watch it!

